<h3>Where the quiz was right about the original</h3>
<p>A fresh model answered a quiz on Fortress from its training alone (part 4). The audit graded each answer against today's tree, so an answer can be right about the team's Fortress and still be graded down:</p>
<ul>
<li>The quiz didn't say how the number types relate. A reader who assumes a tower, narrower below wider, describes the team's interpreter library exactly.</li>
<li>Its answer that overflow raises an error, graded correct, holds for the team's specification and compiler. The team's walk wrapped.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some answers marked wrong come from the team's own specification, which describes more than the team built:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Monoid[\T, ⊕\]</code> with laws: a chapter of the specification. The library has it only in a comment and in its incomplete folder.</li>
<li><code>fortress run Foo.fss</code>: the specification's overview says this. The team's tool runs a program under walk with <code>fortress Foo.fss</code>.</li>
<li>Complex numbers: named in the specification, in no library.</li>
<li>Tests that state their expected output: the team's compiler tests do this. Its interpreter tests don't.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two answers are wrong for both the team and the revival: integer types that wrap by themselves (the specification wraps only with its own operators), and an <code>Any</code> type that doesn't hold tuples. One more is wrong for the team: the guess that walk built part of the coercion rules. The team's walk had none.</p>
